# Walter telemetry (`/srv/telemetry`)

Live dashboard for the inference stack, published as https://${SPARK_TELEMETRY_HOST}. Covenant handles TLS and
Pocket-ID OIDC (oauth2-proxy + nginx) and proxies to `http://${BACKEND_WG_IP}:3200` over WireGuard.

| Path | What it does |
|---|---|
| `GET /` | dashboard SPA (vanilla JS, inline SVG, self-hosted OFL fonts, strict CSP) |
| `GET /api/v1/telemetry` | `{viewer, history, snapshot}`: 30 min history from range queries plus the current snapshot |
| `GET /api/v1/stream` | SSE `event: snapshot` every ~2 s, plus a `: hb` comment every 15 s |
| `GET /healthz` | `ok` |

## Layout
```
compose.yaml                 app (host network, uid 10001, read-only, cap_drop ALL) + backup-status sidecar
build/Dockerfile             python:3.13-alpine pinned by digest; deps pinned in build/requirements.txt
build/app/main.py            collector + HTTP (fixed server-side PromQL; the client never sends a query)
build/app/static/            index.html, app.css, app.js, fonts/ (Orbitron, Inter, JetBrains Mono + OFL licenses)
secrets/llama-swap-api-key   copy of /etc/llama-swap/api-key (root:10001 0440), re-creatable
```
The nightly backup's config tarball covers all of `/srv/telemetry` (`secrets/` included, like every `/srv/*/`; see
`${MODELS_DIR}/backups/RESTORE.md`). The dashboard keeps no state of its own.

Tests live in the repo only (`walter/telemetry/tests`, not deployed): `python3 -m pytest walter/telemetry/tests -q`.
They need starlette, httpx and uvicorn installed locally; otherwise they are skipped.

## How it works
- One collector loop. llama-swap `/running` is polled every 2 s, the fast PromQL set every 4 s (Prometheus scrapes every 15 s),
  the slow set (latency, 1 h totals, per-key today, probes, up, alerts) every 15 s, and range queries every 30 s.
  The JSON is serialised once per cycle and every HTTP/SSE client gets the same bytes, so N viewers add no Prometheus load.
- Host networking is used because Prometheus listens only on 127.0.0.1:9090 (host network). There is no monitoring docker
  network to join. The app binds ${BACKEND_WG_IP}:3200 itself (IP_FREEBIND, so it starts even before wg0 is up).
- `backup-status` (alpine, root without capabilities, no network, read-only) reads `${MODELS_DIR}/backups/LAST_OK` every 60 s
  and writes only `{ts, name}` to a tmpfs volume.
- PCIe link width and speed come from `/sys/bus/pci/devices/<bus>/current_link_*` and `max_link_*`, because DCGM
  doesn't export them on GeForce. The server builds the GPU card's link chip (`pcie.label`, `pcie.level`, `pcie.title` in
  the snapshot) and the browser only displays it:
  - `PCIe Gen4 x8`: the current generation and width. A link narrower than the card's maximum (x8 of x16 on a
    bifurcated slot, say) is mentioned in the tooltip only. The app knows nothing about slots or chipsets and claims nothing.
  - `· power-save` (muted): the link runs below its maximum generation. GPUs drop to Gen1/2 at idle and go back to Gen4 under
    load. This is normal.
  - `· below x8` (yellow): the width is below `TELEMETRY_GPU_EXPECTED_WIDTH` (see Configuration). This is the only warning,
    and with the setting unset there is none.
- X-Forwarded-User/Email are only displayed, after being sanitised. They are never used for authorisation.

## Configuration
Environment of the `app` service in `compose.yaml` (change it there, then `sudo docker compose up -d`):

| Variable | Value in compose | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `BIND` | `${BACKEND_WG_IP}:3200` | listen address (bound with `IP_FREEBIND`) |
| `PROM_URL` | `http://127.0.0.1:9090` | Prometheus (host network, loopback only) |
| `LLAMA_SWAP_URL` | `http://${BACKEND_WG_IP}:8080` | llama-swap, for `/running` |
| `LLAMA_SWAP_KEY_FILE` | `/run/secrets/llama-swap-api-key` | read on every poll |
| `BACKUP_STATUS_FILE` | `/state/backup.json` | written by the `backup-status` sidecar |
| `TZ_DISPLAY` | `UTC` | "today" boundary for the per-key breakdown |

`TELEMETRY_GPU_EXPECTED_WIDTH` is optional. It sets the PCIe width each GPU should train at: `8` for every GPU, or
`0=16,1=8` per GPU index. The chip warns when a link is narrower than that. Set it in `site.env`; it is rendered into
`build/app/main.py` as the default, and an environment variable of the same name on the `app` service overrides it.
Leave it out of `site.env` (or commented) for no warning. Don't set it to an empty value, because `render.sh` refuses
templates whose site variables are empty. Current riser topology: both GPUs are on CPU root ports at x8, so `8` fits.

Optional cadence knobs (seconds, app defaults): `PUBLISH_INTERVAL` 2, `PROM_FAST_INTERVAL` 4, `SLOW_INTERVAL` 15, `HISTORY_INTERVAL` 30.

## Firewall
- ufw: `ALLOW IN on wg0 from ${EDGE_WG_IP} to ${BACKEND_WG_IP} port 3200/tcp` (from `ufw-rules.sh`). This is the effective gate, because
  host-network traffic goes through INPUT.
- `/usr/local/sbin/docker-user-rules.sh`: 3200 is in `PORTS` (WALTER-PUBLISHED), in case the app is ever switched to a
  docker-published port.

## Operations
```bash
cd /srv/telemetry
sudo docker compose ps                 # both (healthy)
sudo docker compose up -d --build      # after editing build/
sudo docker compose logs -f app
curl -fsS http://${BACKEND_WG_IP}:3200/healthz   # ok
# llama-swap key rotated:
sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g 10001 /etc/llama-swap/api-key secrets/llama-swap-api-key   # read on every poll
```

`walter/deploy.sh` runs `docker compose up -d --build` here on **every** run. Even when every build layer is cached, the
rebuilt image gets a new ID, so `telemetry-app` is recreated (a few seconds of dashboard reconnects). The deploy's dry run
does not show this.

## Troubleshooting
| Symptom | Check / fix |
|---|---|
| dashboard shows no model state, or `llama-swap` errors in the app log | the key copy is stale after a rotation: re-copy it (above) |
| GPU / request panels empty | Prometheus down or targets missing: see `/srv/monitoring/README.md` |
| backup tile stale | `sudo docker compose ps backup-status` (healthy?) and `${MODELS_DIR}/backups/LAST_OK` |
| 502 / 504 at `https://${SPARK_TELEMETRY_HOST}` | the app or the tunnel is down: `curl http://${BACKEND_WG_IP}:3200/healthz` from Covenant, `sudo wg show` on both sides |

## Rollback
A config change: revert the file (or the repo revision) and run `sudo docker compose up -d --build`.

Remove the app (telemetry is part of the base Walter deploy, so also drop it from `walter/` and the firewall templates,
or the next `walter/deploy.sh` run puts it back):
```bash
cd /srv/telemetry && sudo docker compose down -v && sudo docker image rm walter-telemetry:local
sudo ufw delete allow in on wg0 proto tcp from ${EDGE_WG_IP} to ${BACKEND_WG_IP} port 3200
sudo sed -i 's/^PORTS="3000 1411 4000 3200"/PORTS="3000 1411 4000"/' /usr/local/sbin/docker-user-rules.sh
sudo systemctl restart docker-user-rules.service && sudo iptables -S WALTER-PUBLISHED | grep -c 3200   # 0
sudo rm -rf /srv/telemetry
```
Then remove the telemetry vhost and oauth2-proxy config on Covenant (see `covenant/README.md`).
